[1;36r[21;12H[?12l[?25h[?25l [?12l[?25h[?25l [?12l[?25h[?25l [?12l[?25h[?25l [?12l[?25h[?25l [?12l[?25h[?25l [?12l[?25h[?25l [?12l[?25h[?25l [?12l[?25h[?25l [?12l[?25h[?25l [?12l[?25h[?25l[K[?12l[?25h[?25l[2d---[K[3d[K[4d[36m## Defect 3[6d[39m(B[m**Symptom:**[K[8dThe backend and gateway pods were failing their readiness checks. `kubectl describe pod` showed the probe failing with `connection refused` on port 8080, while the ap(B[0;7m>[10;3H(B[mCause:**[K[12dThe chart never set the `PORT` environment variable, so the application used its default port, 8081. The readiness probe and Service were configured for port 8080, so(B[0;7m>[13;1H(B[m[K[14d**Fix:**[K[15d[K[16dI added `PORT: "8080"` under the `env:` block for the backend in `broken-chart/values.yaml`:[K[17d[K[18d```[K[19denv:[20dPORT: "8080"[21d```[22d[K[23dI chose this over changing the port in the chart because the Service and probes were already set to 8080. Changing the app's port keeps the rest of the configuration (B[0;7m>[24;1H(B[m[K[25dThe gateway has the same problem and needs the same change. At the time of writing, I had not yet confirmed the gateway fix. This is still in progress.[26d[K[27d**How I found it:**[28d[K[29d1. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab describe pod` and saw the readiness probe failing with `connection refused` on port 8080.[30d2. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab logs` and saw the app listening on `:8081`.[31d3. Ran `grep -rn -i "port"` on `values.yaml` and `backend.yaml`, which showed the probe using `common.port` (8080) and no `PORT` variable set.[32d4. Added `PORT: "8080"` to the backend's env block, then ran `./scenario.sh up`.[33d5. Confirmed with the backend log (`/healthz -> 200`) and `./scenario.sh verify`, which now passes the backend check.[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[2S[1;36r[32;1H---[33d[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[4S[1;36r[30;1H[36