.yaml`:[32d- Before: `restartPolicy: Always`[33d- After: `restartPolicy: Never`[2d[?12l[?25h[?25l[8d[?12l[?25h[?25l[17d[?12l[?25h[?25l[27d[?12l[?25h[?25l[2;34r[34;1H[6S[1;36r[28;1H[K[29dI chose `Never` because each retry creates a new pod, instead of restarting the same container. The number of retries is controlled by `backoffLimit`.[31d**How I found it:**[33d1. Ran `./scenario.sh up` and got the migrate Job error.[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[9S[1;36r[25;1H2. Read the error message, which named `restartPolicy` and listed the valid values.[26d3. Ran `cat -n broken-chart/templates/migrate-job.yaml` to see the file with line numbers.[27d4. Found the wrong value on line 16 and changed it from `Always` to `Never`.[28d5. Confirmed the change with `grep -n restartPolicy broken-chart/templates/migrate-job.yaml`.[29d---[31d[36m## Defect 2[33d[39m(B[m**Symptom:**[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[15S[1;36r[20;1HAfter deploying the lab, I ran `kubectl -n debug-lab get pods`. All app pods and the migrate Job were stuck in `CreateContainerConfigError`. The events showed this er(B[0;7m>[22;1H(B[m```[23dError: container has runAsNonRoot and image has non-numeric user (nonroot), cannot verify user is non-root[24d```[26d**Cause:**[28dThe Helm templates set `runAsNonRoot: true` but did not set a numeric user ID. The image's user is the name `nonroot`, which is UID `65532`. Kubernetes needs a number(B[0;7m>[30;1H(B[m**Fix:**[32dI added `runAsUser: 65532` under `runAsNonRoot: true` in six templates: `backend.yaml`, `gateway.yaml`, `worker.yaml`, `reporter.yaml`, `metrics.yaml` and `migrate-jo(B[0;7m>[33;1H(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[6S[1;36r[28;1HBefore:[30d```[31drunAsNonRoot: true[32d```[33d[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[6S[1;36r[28;1HAfter:[30d```[31drunAsNonRoot: true[32drunAsUser: 65532[33d```[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[4S[1;36r[31;1HI used the numeric UID because `runAsUser` needs a number, not a username.[33dI also deleted and recreated the migrate Job, because Kubernetes does not allow changes to an existing Job's pod template. Helm could not update it in place.[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[4S[1;36r[31;1H**How I found it:**[33d1. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab get pods` and saw `CreateContainerConfigError`.[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[4S[1;36r[30;1H2. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp` and found the `runAsNonRoot ... non-numeric user` message.[31d3. Ran `grep -rn -i "runAs\|securityContext" broken-chart/` and saw that each template set `runAsNonRoot` with no user ID.[32d4. Ran `docker pull` and `docker inspect` on the image, which showed its user is `nonroot`.[33d5. Ran `docker create`, `docker cp` and `tar` to read `/etc/passwd` inside the image, which showed `nonroot:x:65532:65532`.[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[4S[1;36r[30;1H6. Added `runAsUser: 65532` to the six templates and checked with `grep -rn runAsUser broken-chart/`, which showed six matches.[31d7. Deleted the old migrate Job with `kubectl -n debug-lab delete job migrate`, then ran `./scenario.sh up`.[32d---[33d[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[3S[1;36r[31;1H[36m## Defect 3[33d[39m(B[m**Symptom:**[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[3S[1;36r[32;1HThe backend and gateway pods were failing their readiness checks. `kubectl describe pod` showed the probe failing with `connection refused` on port 8080, while the ap(B[0;7m>[33;1H(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[3S[1;36r[31;1H**Cause:**[33dThe chart never set the `PORT` environment variable, so the application used its default port, 8081. The readiness probe and Service were configured for port 8080, so(B[0;7m>[33;1H(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d
[1;36r[33;1H[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[16S[1;36r[18;1H**Fix:**[20dI added `PORT: "8080"` under the `env:` block for the backend in `broken-chart/values.yaml`:[22d```[23denv:[24dPORT: "8080"[25d```[27dI chose this over changing the port in the chart because the Service and probes were already set to 8080. Changing the app's port keeps the rest of the configuration (B[0;7m>[29;1H(B[mThe gateway has the same problem and needs the same change. At the time of writing, I had not yet confirmed the gateway fix. This is still in progress.[31d**How I found it:**[33d1. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab describe pod` and saw the readiness probe failing with `connection refused` on port 8080.[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[10S[1;36r[24;1H2. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab logs` and saw the app listening on `:8081`.[25d3. Ran `grep -rn -i "port"` on `values.yaml` and `backend.yaml`, which showed the probe using `common.port` (8080) and no `PORT` variable set.[26d4. Added `PORT: "8080"` to the backend's env block, then ran `./scenario.sh up`.[27d5. Confirmed with the backend log (`/healthz -> 200`) and `./scenario.sh verify`, which now passes the backend check.[28d---[30d[36m## Defect 4[32d[39m(B[m**Symptom:**[33d[?12l[?25h[?25l[16;24r[24;1H[3S[1;36r[23;34r[34;1H[3S[1;36r[3;15r[3;1H[6T[1;36r[2;1HAfter the port fix, the gateway's readiness check failed. The gateway log showed:[K[4d```[5dreadiness failed: backend not reachable: GET http://backend.default.svc:8080/healthz: ... lookup backend.default.svc on 10.96.0.10:53: no such host[6d```[8d`verify` also reported `gateway /status does not report backend=ok`.[12;5Hgateway's `BACKEND_URL` in `broken-chart/values.yaml` pointed at namespace `default`, but the backend runs in namespace `debug-lab`. DNS couldn't resolve a host i[16dI changed the namespace in `BACKEND_URL` from `default` to `debug-lab`, so the value is `http://backend.debug-lab.svc:8080`. I kept the rest of the URL the same, sinc(B[0;7m>[20;30H(B[mlogs <gateway-pod> --tail=5` and saw the `no such host` error.[K[21;5Head the hostname in the error (`backend.default.svc`) and compared it with the namespace the lab runs in (`debug-lab`).[22d3. Ran `grep -n -A6 "^gateway:" broken-chart/values.yaml` and found `BACKEND_URL` on line 39.[23;4HChanged the namespace, ran `./scenario.sh up`, then `./scenario.sh verify`. The check `gateway /status reports backend=ok` passed.[24d[K[27;11H[36m5[31d[39m(B[mThe metrics pod was never created. `kubectl -n debug-lab get events` showed:[33d```[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[8S[1;36r[26;1Hreplicaset/metrics-...  (combined from similar events): Error creating: pods "metrics-..." is forbidden: maximum cpu usage per Container is 1, but limit is 4[27d```[29d**Cause:**[31dThe metrics block in `broken-chart/values.yaml` set a CPU request of `"2"` and a CPU limit of `"4"`. The cluster's LimitRange in `cluster-state/limits.yaml` caps each(B[0;7m>[33;1H(B[m**Fix:**[?12l[?25h[?25l[2d[K[3dThe metrics pod was never created. `kubectl -n debug-lab get events` showed:[4d[K[5d```[6dreplicaset/metrics-...  (combined from similar events): Error creating: pods "metrics-..." is forbidden: maximum cpu usage per Container is 1, but limit is 4[7d```[8d[K[9d**Cause:**[10d[K[11dThe metrics block in `broken-chart/values.yaml` set a CPU request of `"2"` and a CPU limit of `"4"`. The cluster's LimitRange in `cluster-state/limits.yaml` caps each(B[0;7m>[12;1H(B[m[K[13d**Fix:**[K[14d[K[15dI changed the metrics CPU request to `50m` and the limit to `200m`, matching the other services in the same file. I didn't change `cluster-state/limits.yaml`, because(B[0;7m>[17;1H(B[m**How I found it:**[19d1. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -10` and saw the `forbidden` message.[20d2. Ran `grep -n -A10 "^metrics:" broken-chart/values.yaml` and found the CPU v