read-only root filesystem in place. The app gets exactly one writable folder, and it's temporary, wh(B[0;7m>[31;1H(B[mI also added `PORT: "8080"` to the worker's `env:` block in `values.yaml`, since the worker was listening on 8081, like the others (see Defect 3).[33d**How I found it:**[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[5S[1;36r[30;1H1. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab get pods -l app=worker` and saw `CrashLoopBackOff`.[31d2. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab logs worker-<pod> --previous` and found the read-only filesystem error.[32d3. Ran `cat -n broken-chart/templates/worker.yaml` to see the container and pod structure.[33d4. Added the volume and mount. My first attempts had the wrong indentation, which Helm reported as a YAML parse error. I used `awk` to measure the indentation of the (B[0;7m>[33;1H(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[4S[1;36r[30;1H5. Ran `./scenario.sh up` and checked the worker with `kubectl -n debug-lab logs` on the new pod. It started without the cache error, then passed `verify` after the `(B[0;7m>[31;1H(B[m---[33dIf you ran out of time on any defect, say so here and describe what you would[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[2S[1;36r[32;1Hhave tried next — that section is read carefully and counts in your favour.[33d[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[?25l[31d[?12l[?25h[?25l[28d[?12l[?25h[?25l[26d[?12l[?25h[?25l[23d[?12l[?25h[?25l[21d[?12l[?25h[?25l[18d[?12l[?25h[?25l[A[?12l[?25h[?25l[15d[?12l[?25h[?25l[A[?12l[?25h[?25l[A[?12l[?25h[?25l[10d[?12l[?25h[?25l[2;33r[2;1H[3T[1;36r[2;1H**Symptom:**[4dThe worker pod was in `CrashLoopBackOff`. The previous container's log said:[2d[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;33r8[7T[1;36r[2;1H2. Ran `grep -n -A10 "^metrics:" broken-chart/values.yaml` and found the CPU values `"2"` and `"4"`.[3d3. Compared them with `cluster-state/limits.yaml`, which sets `max: cpu: "1"`.[4d4. Changed the values, ran `./scenario.sh up` and `./scenario.sh verify`. The metrics deployment check then passed.[7d[36m## Defect 6[2d[39m(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;33r8[4T[1;36r[3;1H**How I found it:**[5d1. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -10` and saw the `forbidden` message.[2d[?12l[?25h[?25l[35d[J[36d[?12l[?25h[36;1H[?1049l[23;0;0t[?1l>[?2004l]0;bcapdevops@solvervsvr054: ~/eb-devops-takehome/lab[01;32mbcapdevops@solvervsvr054[00m:[01;34m~/eb-devops-takehome/lab[00m$ nano FINDINGS.md 
[?2004h[?1049h[22;0;0t[1;36r(B[m[4l[?7h[39;49m[?1h=[?1h=[?25l[39;49m(B[m[H[2J[34;77H(B[0;7m[ Reading... ](B[m[34;75H(B[0;7m[ Read 213 lines ](B[m[H(B[0;7m  GNU nano 4.8                                                                  FINDINGS.md                                                                            [1;166H(B[m[35d(B[0;7m^G(B[m Get Help     (B[0;7m^O(B[m Write Out    (B[0;7m^W(B[m Where Is     (B[0;7m^K(B[m Cut Text     (B[0;7m^J(B[m Justify[81G(B[0;7m^C(B[m Cur Pos[97G(B[0;7mM-U(B[m Undo[35;113H(B[0;7mM-A(B[m Mark Text   (B[0;7mM-](B[m To Bracket  (B[0;7mM-Q(B[m Previous[36d(B[0;7m^X(B[m Exit[36;17H(B[0;7m^R(B[m Read File    (B[0;7m^\(B[m Replace[49G(B[0;7m^U(B[m Paste Text   (B[0;7m^T(B[m To Spell     (B[0;7m^_(B[m Go To Line   (B[0;7mM-E(B[m Redo[36;113H(B[0;7mM-6(B[m Copy Text   (B[0;7m^Q(B[m Where Was    (B[0;7mM-W(B[m Next[34d[2d[39;49m[36m# Findings — Part 4 debug lab[4d[39m(B[mFill in one entry per defect you find. Paste the *actual* output you saw —[5dwe cross-check it against your session recording and your git diff, and the[6ddiagnostic path matters more to us than the fix itself.[8dBefore you start investigating, begin recording:[9d`script -q part4-session.log` (or `asciinema rec part4-session.cast`), and[10dcommit that file alongside this one.[12d---[14d[36m## Defect 1[16d[39m(B[m**Symptom:**[18dWhen I ran `./scenario.sh up`, the migration Job failed with this error:[20d```[21dJob.batch "migrate" is invalid: spec.template.spec.restartPolicy: Required value: valid values: "OnFailure", "Never"[22d```[24d**Cause:**[26dThe migrate Job's `restartPolicy` was set to `Always` at line 16 of `broken-chart/templates/migrate-job.yaml`. Kubernetes Jobs only accept `Never` or `OnFailure`, so (B[0;7m>[28;1H(B[m**Fix:**[30dChanged the value in `broken-chart/templates/migrate-job.yaml`:[32d- Before: `restartPolicy: Always`[33d- After: `restartPolicy: Never`[2d[?12l[?25h[?25l[9d[?12l[?25h[?25l[18d[?12l[?25h[?25l[21d[?12l[?25h[?25l[2;34r[34;1H
[1;36r[33;1H[K[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[4S[1;36r[30;1HI chose `Never` because each retry creates a new pod, instead of restarting the same container. The number of retries is controlled by `backoffLimit`.[32d**How I found it:**[33d[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[15S[1;36r[19;1H1. Ran `./scenario.sh up` and got the migrate Job error.[20d2. Read the error message, which named `restartPolicy` and listed the valid values.[21d3. Ran `cat -n broken-chart/templates/migrate-job.yaml` to see the file with line numbers.[22d4. Found the wrong value on line 16 and changed it from `Always` to `Never`.[23d5. Confirmed the change with `grep -n restartPolicy broken-chart/templates/migrate-job.yaml`.[24d---[26d[36m## Defect 2[28d[39m(B[m**Symptom:**[30dAfter deploying the lab, I ran `kubectl -n debug-lab get pods`. All app pods and the migrate Job were stuck in `CreateContainerConfigError`. The events showed this er(B[0;7m>[32;1H(B[m```[33dError: container has runAsNonRoot and image has non-numeric user (nonroot), cannot verify user is non-root[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[10S[1;36r[24;1H```[26d**Cause:**[28dThe Helm templates set `runAsNonRoot: true` but did not set a numeric user ID. The image's user is the name `nonroot`, which is UID `65532`. Kubernetes needs a number(B[0;7m>[30;1H(B[m**Fix:**[32dI added `runAsUser: 65532` under `runAsNonRoot: true` in six templates: `backend.yaml`, `gateway.yaml`, `worker.yaml`, `reporter.yaml`, `metrics.yaml` and `migrate-jo(B[0;7m>[33;1H(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[9S[1;36r[25;1HBefore:[27d```[28drunAsNonRoot: true[29d```[31dAfter:[33d```[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[7S[1;36r[27;1HrunAsNonRoot: true[28drunAsUser: 65532[29d```[31dI used the numeric UID because `runAsUser` needs a number, not a username.[33dI also deleted and recreated the migrate Job, because Kubernetes does not allow changes to an existing Job's pod template. Helm could not update it in place.[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[6S[1;36r[29;1H**How I found it:**[31d1. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab get pods` and saw `CreateContainerConfigError`.[32d2. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp` and found the `runAsNonRoot ... non-numeric user` message.[33d3. Ran `grep -rn -i "runAs\|securityContext" broken-chart/` and saw that each template set `runAsNonRoot` with no user ID.[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[5S[1;36r[29;1H4. Ran `docker pull` and `docker inspect` on the image, which showed its user is `nonroot`.[30d5. Ran `docker create`, `docker cp` and `tar` to read `/etc/passwd` inside the image, which showed `nonroot:x:65532:65532`.[31d6. Added `runAsUser: 65532` to the six templates and checked with `grep -rn runAsUser broken-chart/`, which showed six matches.[32d7. Deleted the old migrate Job with `kubectl -n debug-lab delete job migrate`, then ran `./scenario.sh up`.[33d---[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[5S[1;36r[30;1H[36m## Defect 3[32d[39m(B[m**Symptom:**[33d[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[4S[1;36r[30;1HThe backend and gateway pods were failing their readiness checks. `kubectl describe pod` showed the probe failing with `connection refused` on port 8080, while the ap(B[0;7m>[32;1H(B[m**Cause:**[33d[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[4S[1;36r[30;1HThe chart never set the `PORT` environment variable, so the application used its default port, 8081. The readiness probe and Service were configured for port 8080, so(B[0;7m>[32;1H(B[m**Fix:**[33d[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[3S[1;36r[31;1HI added `PORT: "8080"` under the `env:` block for the backend in `broken-chart/values.yaml`:[33d```[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[3S[1;36r[31;1Henv:[32dPORT: "8080"[33d```[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[3S[1;36r[32;1HI chose this over changing the port in the chart because the Service and probes were already set to 8080. Changing the app's port keeps the rest of the configuration (B[0;7m>[33;1H(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[3S[1;36r[31;1HThe gateway has the same problem and needs the same change. At the time of writing, I had not yet confirmed the gateway fix. This is still in progress.[33d**How I found it:**[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d
[1;36r[33;1H[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[10S[1;36r[24;1H1. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab describe pod` and saw the readiness probe failing with `connection refused` on port 8080.[25d2. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab logs` and saw the app listening on `:8081`.[26d3. Ran `grep -rn -i "port"` on `values.yaml` and `backend.yaml`, which showed the probe using `common.port` (8080) and no `PORT` variable set.[27d4. Added `PORT: "8080"` to the backend's env block, then ran `./scenario.sh up`.[28d5. Confirmed with the backend log (`/healthz -> 200`) and `./scenario.sh verify`, which now passes the backend check.[29d---[31d[36m## Defect 4[33d[39m(B[m**Symptom:**[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[16S[1;36r[19;1HAfter the port fix, the gateway's readiness check failed. The gateway log showed:[21d```[22dreadiness failed: backend not reachable: GET http://backend.default.svc:8080/healthz: ... lookup backend.default.svc on 10.96.0.10:53: no such host[23d```[25d`verify` also reported `gateway /status does not report backend=ok`.[27d**Cause:**[29dThe gateway's `BACKEND_URL` in `broken-chart/values.yaml` pointed at namespace `default`, but the backend runs in namespace `debug-lab`. DNS couldn't resolve a host i(B[0;7m>[31;1H(B[m**Fix:**[33dI changed the namespace in `BACKEND_URL` from `default` to `debug-lab`, so the value is `http://backend.debug-lab.svc:8080`. I kept the rest of the URL the same, sinc(B[0;7m>[33;1H(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[15S[1;36r[20;1H**How I found it:**[22d1. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab logs <gateway-pod> --tail=5` and saw the `no such host` error.[23d2. Read the hostname in the error (`backend.default.svc`) and compared it with the namespace the lab runs in (`debug-lab`).[24d3. Ran `grep -n -A6 "^gateway:" broken-chart/values.yaml` and found `BACKEND_URL` on line 39.[25d4. Changed the namespace, ran `./scenario.sh up`, then `./scenario.sh verify`. The check `gateway /status reports backend=ok` passed.[27d---[29d[36m## Defect 5[31d[39m(B[m**Symptom:**[33dThe metrics pod was never created. `kubectl -n debug-lab get events` showed:[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[9S[1;36r[26;1H```[27dreplicaset/metrics-...  (combined from similar events): Error creating: pods "metrics-..." is forbidden: maximum cpu usage per Container is 1, but limit is 4[28d```[30d**Cause:**[32dThe metrics block in `broken-chart/values.yaml` set a CPU request of `"2"` and a CPU limit of `"4"`. The cluster's LimitRange in `cluster-state/limits.yaml` caps each(B[0;7m>[33;1H(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[15S[1;36r[19;1H**Fix:**[21dI changed the metrics CPU request to `50m` and the limit to `200m`, matching the other services in the same file. I didn't change `cluster-state/limits.yaml`, because(B[0;7m>[23;1H(B[m**How I found it:**[25d1. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -10` and saw the `forbidden` message.[26d2. Ran `grep -n -A10 "^metrics:" broken-chart/values.yaml` and found the CPU values `"2"` and `"4"`.[27d3. Compared them with `cluster-state/limits.yaml`, which sets `max: cpu: "1"`.[28d4. Changed the values, ran `./scenario.sh up` and `./scenario.sh verify`. The metrics deployment check then passed.[31d[36m## Defect 6[33d[39m(B[m**Symptom:**[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[6S[1;36r[29;1HThe worker pod was in `CrashLoopBackOff`. The previous container's log said:[31d```[32dFATAL: worker could not initialise its cache: mkdir /var/cache/app: read-only file system[33d```[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[9S[1;36r[26;1H**Cause:**[28dThe worker's container sets `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true` in `broken-chart/templates/worker.yaml`, which makes the whole filesystem read-only. The app needs to creat(B[0;7m>[30;1H(B[m**Fix:**[32dI kept `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true`, since it's a good security setting, and gave the app a writable place to store its cache. In `broken-chart/templates/worker.yam(B[0;7m>[33;1H(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[4S[1;36r[30;1H- a `volumes:` entry under the pod spec, with a volume named `cache` backed by `emptyDir: {}`[31d- a `volumeMounts:` entry under the container, mounting `cache` at `/var/cache/app`[33dI chose this over setting `CACHE_DIR` because the mount keeps the read-only root filesystem in place. The app gets exactly one writable folder, and it's temporary, wh(B[0;7m>[33;1H(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[3S[1;36r[32;1HI also added `PORT: "8080"` to the worker's `env:` block in `values.yaml`, since the worker was listening on 8081, like the others (see Defect 3).[33d[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[3S[1;36r[31;1H**How I found it:**[33d1. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab get pods -l app=worker` and saw `CrashLoopBackOff`.[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[3S[1;36r[31;1H2. Ran `kubectl -n debug-lab logs worker-<pod> --previous` and found the read-only filesystem error.[32d3. Ran `cat -n broken-chart/templates/worker.yaml` to see the container and pod structure.[33d4. Added the volume and mount. My first attempts had the wrong indentation, which Helm reported as a YAML parse error. I used `awk` to measure the indentation of the (B[0;7m>[33;1H(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[3S[1;36r[31;1H5. Ran `./scenario.sh up` and checked the worker with `kubectl -n debug-lab logs` on the new pod. It started without the cache error, then passed `verify` after the `(B[0;7m>[32;1H(B[m---[33d[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d[2S[1;36r[32;1HIf you ran out of time on any defect, say so here and describe what you would[33dhave tried next — that section is read carefully and counts in your favour.[?12l[?25h[?25l7[2;34r8[34d
